 Read-only project lessons from CIT, cardassia, and sporc add an operational test: \textbf{verify the code actually imported and the workflow actually completed}. Relevant failures include repeated JAX compilation under a production resource limit, an environment importing a different checkout, a shared branch moving between validation and delivery, and a completed long job whose follow-on step never ran. These records motivate import-path and revision checks, repeated-call tests, scheduler-aware execution receipts, and revalidation on the delivered revision. They are development lessons from restricted sources, not newly reproduced campaigns or claims that all remaining validation gaps are closed.
 
+\subsection{Star--black-hole encounter dynamics}
+The main dynamics paper on star--black-hole encounters provides another example of reviewing the scientific measure, its numerical implementation, and the run that supplies the manuscript~\cite{StarBHDynamics}. Its development records support three practical lessons:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Follow the complete estimator.} Review of the Hill encounter kernel traced incoming flux through proposal and inclusion weights, the denominator counting all proposals, and the conditional encounter distribution. An apparent normalization discrepancy canceled elsewhere in the population calculation. Changing an isolated prefactor would have changed a consistent rate. Analytic limiting cases and archived solver comparisons supplied additional checks.
+\item \textbf{Diagnose a changed regression before replacing it.} Seeded output hashes differed across dependency versions. Matched-environment comparisons of historical and newer code agreed, while a fixed-interpreter comparison isolated a floating-point difference in the mapping of uniform random draws across NumPy versions. Matching random bits did not ensure matching numerical samples. The appropriate response was to investigate the fixture's environment and numerical contract, rather than silently replace its expected hash.
+\item \textbf{Resolve the producing run.} Archived production code, kernel data, and convergence records matched their manifest identities, although a remote checkout's project note still described the initial scaffold. A familiar project directory did not identify the calculation that produced the result. Inspect the actual run artifacts and runtime, then connect them to the manuscript's generated quantities.
+\end{itemize}
+The shared manuscript tooling also rejects conflicting rendered values for a shared result name across generated bundles, preventing TeX input order from silently choosing a number. These checks connect the \texttt{result-provenance}, \texttt{simulation-validation}, and \texttt{adversarial-science-review} practices to a concrete encounter calculation.
+
 \subsection{Adversarial review and human oversight}
 Adversarial review deliberately directs the agents' bug-finding ability at an established result, including one the implementer and human reviewers believe is correct. We distinguish a science review of the claim, assumptions, and domain from a code and numerics review of the implementation and its checks. Both examine a fixed candidate and a stated scientific specification. They seek counterexamples, convention errors, confounded comparisons, and unjustified extrapolations, with enough information to reproduce the objection. A reviewer should know the question and assumptions without being primed by the implementer's preferred explanation of why the answer must be correct.
 
